# v0.9.3 hotfix — exclude editor/backup pollution from tarball
# Caught 6 files in v0.9.2 tarball: license.js.bak.*, license.js.pre-*,
# server.js.pre-*, package.json.pre-* — all are pre-edit safety snapshots.
*.bak
*.bak.*
*.pre-*
*.orig
*.swp
*.tmp

# [packguard 2026-08-06 CTO] MEASURED, not assumed: npm 11.11.0 FORCE-INCLUDES
# any PACKAGE-ROOT file whose name begins with README or LICENSE (case-sensitive,
# position 0). .npmignore GETS NO VOTE on those names -- the *.pre-* rule above is
# correct and simply UNREACHABLE for them. That is how README.md.pre-ipstrip-* and
# README.md.pre-v095-lock-* reached the PUBLIC npm registry in v0.9.5, carrying the
# PRE-honesty-scrub marketing copy (including a '779 downloads' claim that had been
# deliberately removed from the shipped README).
# The force-include is ROOT-ONLY -- a copy in a subdirectory IS excluded -- so editor
# backups now live in _backups/. DO NOT put editor backups in the package root.
_backups/

# [2026-08-06 v0.9.9 CTO] BINARY ASSETS -- THE OTHER HALF OF A FIX I ONLY
# HALF-MADE, RECORDED HERE SO THE SHAPE IS NOT REPEATED.
# In v0.9.8 I closed the .gitignore/.npmignore divergence in ONE direction: I
# copied this file's backup patterns into .gitignore and wrote in the commit
# message that ".npmignore gets no vote on what git publishes." The converse was
# exactly as true and I did not check it -- .GITIGNORE GETS NO VOTE ON WHAT NPM
# PUBLISHES. hero.png (369,469 bytes) had NEVER been git-tracked (`git log --all
# -- hero.png` empty, control on README.md non-empty), so the .gitignore line I
# added changed nothing at all; the tarball was the only surface it was ever on,
# and the tarball is the surface I did not audit. It shipped in every published
# version through 0.9.8 -- 369 kB of 542 kB unpacked, 68% of every install --
# referenced by ZERO files in the repo (grep across js/json/md/html outside
# node_modules, with a positive control that returned three real files).
# An MCP stdio server renders nothing and needs no images. Site artwork lives in
# the website repo. Verify with `npm pack --dry-run --json`, never by eye.
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.gif
*.webp
*.ico
*.mp4
*.mov
